{"name":"Last Sunlight","hex":"#e5b73b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"last","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Final, ultimate, coming after all others of its kind.","Most recent, latest, last so far."]},{"part":"Determiner","definitions":["The (one) immediately before the present.","Closest in the past, or closest but one if the closest was very recent; of days, sometimes thought to specifically refer to the instance closest to seven days (one week) ago, or the most recent instance before seven days (one week) ago."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/last"},{"term":"sunlight","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["All the electromagnetic radiation given off by the Sun; especially, that in the visible spectrum and that bathes the Earth.","Brightness, hope; a positive outlook."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To work on the side (at a secondary job) during the daytime."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sunlight"}],"entries":[{"title":"Last","blocks":[{"text":"A last is a mechanical form used by shoemakers and cordwainers in the manufacture and repair of shoes. Lasts come in many styles and sizes, depending on the exact job they are designed for. Common variations include simple one-size lasts used for repairing soles and heels, custom-purpose mechanized lasts used in modern mass production, and custom-made lasts used in the making of bespoke footwear. Lasts are made of firm materials—hardwoods, cast iron, and high-density plastics—to withstand contact with wetted leather and the strong forces involved in reshaping it. Since the early 19th century, lasts typically come in pairs to match the separate shapes of the right and left feet. The development of an automated lasting machine by the Surinamese-American Jan Ernst Matzeliger in the 1880s was a major development in shoe production, immediately improving quality, halving prices, and eliminating the previous putting-out systems surrounding shoemaking centers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/PikiWiki_Israel_13497_shoe_trees.JPG/330px-PikiWiki_Israel_13497_shoe_trees.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Last","wikidata":"Q1069931"},{"title":"Sunlight","blocks":[{"text":"Sunlight is the portion of the electromagnetic radiation which is emitted by the Sun and received by the Earth, in particular the visible light perceptible to the human eye as well as invisible infrared and ultraviolet lights. However, according to the American Meteorological Society, there are \"conflicting conventions as to whether all three [...] are referred to as light, or whether that term should only be applied to the visible portion of the spectrum\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/STS-134_EVA4_view_to_the_Russian_Orbital_Segment.jpg/330px-STS-134_EVA4_view_to_the_Russian_Orbital_Segment.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunlight","wikidata":"Q193788"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg/500px-Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg/500px-Schuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wooden lasts in a shoemaker's workshop in Hamburg, Germany","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASchuhmacherwerkstatt_HH.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif/500px-Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif/500px-Jan_ernst_matzeliger.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jan Ernst Matzeliger in 1885","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJan_ernst_matzeliger.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Zwikmachine_1885.jpg/250px-Zwikmachine_1885.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4f/Zwikmachine_1885.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Zwikmachine_1885.jpg/250px-Zwikmachine_1885.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Matzeliger's lasting machine, complex enough the patent office had to send an agent to Lynn to see it in operation","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZwikmachine_1885.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg/500px-Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg/1280px-Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg/500px-Museo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The personal lasts of Audrey Hepburn and Ava Gardner at the Ferragamo Museum in Florence, Italy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMuseo_Ferragamo_-_forme.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Shoemakers_tripartite_metal_last_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg/330px-Shoemakers_tripartite_metal_last_pre_1950s_Gothenburg_Sweden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/